---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { published, schemaPath, schemas, vocabulary } from '../lib/publish';
import { release } from '../lib/library';

// The current release first, then every earlier version, newest first.
const current = published.filter((p) => p.latest);
const earlier = published.filter((p) => !p.latest).reverse();
import { formatDate } from '../lib/format';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
---
<Base title="Data" description="Download the library's packages, catalog, and schema as JSON.">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Data</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every page on this site is generated from versioned JSON packages. You can download them for your own
        tools, and each one has a SHA-256 digest you can check. The library is released as a whole, so every
        package in a release has its version. This is release {release}.
      </p>
    </header>

    {[current, earlier].map((list, i) => (
    <>
    {i === 1 && <h2 class="earlier">Earlier versions</h2>}
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="stack-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Package</th>
            <th scope="col">Version</th>
            <th scope="col">Published</th>
            <th scope="col">License</th>
            <th scope="col">Download</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {list.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                {p.data.title}
                {p.data.dependencies?.map((d) => (
                  <p class="digest">Builds on {published.find((q) => q.data.packageId === d.packageId && q.data.version === d.version)?.data.title ?? d.packageId} {d.version}</p>
                ))}
                <p class="digest">SHA-256 <code>{p.sha256}</code></p>
              </th>
              <td data-label="Version" class="nowrap">{p.data.version}{p.latest && <span class="latest">Latest</span>}</td>
              <td data-label="Published" class="nowrap">{formatDate(p.data.publishedAt)}</td>
              <td data-label="License">{p.data.license}</td>
              <td data-label="Download">
                <div><a href={href(p.path)}>blueprint.json</a></div>
                <div><a href={href(`${p.path}.sha256`)}>blueprint.json.sha256</a></div>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    </>
    ))}

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Catalog</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={href('catalog.json')}><code>catalog.json</code></a> lists every published version of every
        package, with its license and digest. Its <code>release</code> names the current release, whose packages it marks
        <code>latest</code>. Its <code>href</code> values are relative to the catalog's own address, so tools
        can find each package from the catalog alone.
      </p>
      <h2>Vocabulary</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={href('vocabulary.json')}><code>vocabulary.json</code></a> names the
        {Object.keys(vocabulary().types).length} element types and {Object.keys(vocabulary().predicates).length} relationships packages
        use, with their labels and definitions. Each type's <code>page</code> gives the address of an element's page, where
        <code>{'{key}'}</code> is the last part of the element ID.
      </p>
      <h2>Schema</h2>
      <p>
        Packages follow the <a href={href(schemaPath)}>package JSON Schema</a>. A package version never
        changes once it's published; changes go into a new version. The schema is versioned the same way, and
        every version stays published: {schemas.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={href(s.path)}>{s.version}</a></>)}.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since schema 0.2.0, a package can build on others through <code>dependencies</code>, each pinned to a version.
        Its relations and discovery answers can then point at those packages' elements by ID, so shared capabilities
        are described once.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since schema 0.3.0, the schema checks what implementation patterns and solutions say about themselves in
        <code>attributes</code>: a pattern's approach and layer, and lists such as its strengths and what to watch
        for. A <code>candidate_pattern</code> relation can say <code>"coverage": "partial"</code> when the pattern
        covers only part of the capability; without it, the pattern covers it fully. Patterns can also say they
        need another (<code>depends_on</code>) or work well with one (<code>works_with</code>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Since schema 0.4.0, every pattern lists its <code>ways</code> to source it, each with its approach, and a
        <code>depends_on</code> relation with a <code>way</code> is a need of that way only. Each mission has one
        solution, in its own package: its long-term architecture, with <code>principles</code>. Each of its
        <code>includes</code> relations to a pattern with several ways names the <code>way</code> it chose. An
        outcome lists its <code>measures</code> in its <code>attributes</code>, instead of linking to measure elements.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  th[scope='row'] { min-width: 16rem; }
  .prose { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .earlier { margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .digest { margin: .3rem 0 0; font-size: .8125rem; font-weight: 400; color: var(--text-muted); }
  .digest code { font-size: .75rem; }
  .latest { margin-left: .4rem; padding: .05rem .45rem; border-radius: 999px; font-size: .75rem; background: var(--surface-muted); color: var(--text-muted); }
</style>
